es astronomically large.\n    85→\n    86→### Why the parameter change λ: −1/20 → −1/50 didn't fix it\n    87→\n    88→The search agent's reasoning was correct that making |λ| smaller pushes the failure threshold to larger values:\n    89→- Round 2 (λ = −1/20): R_crit = 14751/292... wait, the coefficients change with N. For N = 20: R_crit ≈ 20.5, failure at (1+t)(1+x) > ~10¹³·³\n    90→- Round 3 (λ = −1/50): R_crit ≈ 50.5, failure at (1+t)(1+x) > ~10⁴²·⁶\n    91→\n    92→But the failure is **structural to the entire family** λ = −1/N with the double-log ansatz. Here is why:\n    93→\n    94→For λ = −1/N, the L₂ expression (after normalization) becomes a quadratic in z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² with:\n    95→- z² coefficient: `(const_pos)·N⁴ − (const_pos)·N³·R` where R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(2/N)\n    96→- The z² coefficient flips sign at R_crit ~ O(N)\n    97→- R_crit ~ N means (1+t)(1+x) ~ N^(N/2), which grows super-exponentially with N\n    98→- BUT: for any finite N, there exist t, x, c values making L₂ < 0\n    99→\n   100→The fix cannot come from making N larger within this family. The search agent should consider:\n   101→\n   102→1. **A fundamentally different ansatz for ψ** — the double-log ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x) has the perfect \"self-similar\" property for conditions 1 and the sufficient condition, but its L₂ structure inherently produces an R-dependent negative term that grows faster than the constant positive term.\n   103→\n   104→2. **Modifying the ansatz to suppress the R-dependent term in L₂** — the term involving φ³ · (tilde_square(ψ))² in L₂ generates the problematic −lam⁴·φ³·(tilde_box(ψ))² contribution. An ansatz where tilde_box(ψ) decays faster relative to the other L₂ terms might avoid this.\n   105→\n   106→3. **Exploring ansätze where L₂ is manifestly non-negative** — for instance, if ψ satisfies □ψ = 0 (wave equation) AND ψ̃ = 0, many of the problematic L₂ terms vanish. But this severely constrains ψ and may conflict with condition 2.\n   107→\n   108→4.