{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_01GENCHmEVGQ4hrZ4N1wLbBd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01UqfHWm2uZXeUHLqgSi6Jeu","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"source /home/cyanz/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate wave && python3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport sympy as sp\nimport numpy as np\nimport sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN')\nfrom verify_engine import run_verification, x, t, c, alpha, s, lam\n\n# KEY INSIGHT from the failure analysis:\n# L₂ has a term -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)². With s=-1: -(-1)³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² = λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² > 0.\n# Wait! Let me recheck. s³ = (-1)³ = -1. So -s³ = -(-1) = +1.\n# The term is: -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² = +λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² ≥ 0.\n# That's actually POSITIVE! Let me re-read the L₂ definition carefully.\n\n# From verify_engine.py:\n# L₂ = (1/2)((α-1)sλ·□(φ·□ψ) - sλ²·□(φ·ψ̃))\n#     + s³λ³(α-1)φ³·ψ̃·□ψ - s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²\n#     + s³λ³(∂_t(φ³·ψ̃·ψ_t) + c²∂_x(φ³·ψ̃·ψ_x))\n\n# The problematic term: -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²\n# s = -1, s³ = -1, so -s³ = 1. Thus: -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² = λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² ≥ 0.\n# This is POSITIVE! So it's not the problematic one.\n\n# Let me look at ALL terms:\n# Term A: (1/2)(α-1)sλ·□(φ·□ψ) = (1/2)(α-1)(-1)λ·□(φ·□ψ)\n# Term B: -(1/2)sλ²·□(φ·ψ̃) = (1/2)λ²·□(φ·ψ̃)\n# Term C: s³λ³(α-1)φ³·ψ̃·□ψ = -λ³(α-1)φ³·ψ̃·□ψ\n# Term D: -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² = +λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²  (POSITIVE ✓)\n# Term E: s³λ³(∂_t(φ³·ψ̃·ψ_t) + c²∂_x(φ³·ψ̃·ψ_x))\n#        = -λ³(∂_t(φ³·ψ̃·ψ_t) + c²∂_x(φ³·ψ̃·ψ_x))\n\n# For α = 1: Terms A and C vanish. L₂ = B + D + E.\n# D ≥ 0 always. So the negativity comes from B + E.\n# \n# Let me check what happens with the PURE traveling wave ψ = -log(1+x+ct):\n# □ψ = 0, ψ̃ = 0\n# Term B = (1/2)λ²·□(φ·0) = 0\n# Term D = 0  \n# Term E = -λ³(∂_t(φ³·0) + c²∂_x(φ³·0)) = 0\n# So L₂ = 0 as expected.\n\n# For the double-log ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x):\n# ψ̃ = 1/(1+t)² - c²/(1+x)² ≠ 0\n# The terms B and E involve □(φ·ψ̃) and derivatives of φ³·ψ̃·ψ_x etc.\n# These involve second derivatives of products, which expand to many terms.\n# Some of these expanded terms are negative and grow with φ.\n\n# FUNDAMENTAL OBSERVATION: \n# If we choose s to be a DIFFERENT negative value (not -1), the balance changes!\n# s enters L₂ in specific powers: s (in terms A,B), s³ (in terms C,D,E).\n# For small |s|: terms A,B are O(|s|) while terms C,D,E are O(|s|³).\n# So for small |s|, L₂ ≈ (1/2)(-sλ²)□(φ·ψ̃) = the first-order terms dominate.\n#\n# Can we choose s small enough that term B dominates and is positive?\n# B = (1/2)λ²·□(φ·ψ̃) with the wrong sign convention...\n# Actually B = -(1/2)sλ²·□(φ·ψ̃) = (1/2)|s|λ²·□(φ·ψ̃) (since s<0)\n# Sign of B depends on □(φ·ψ̃). Let me compute this for the double-log.\n\nprint(\"=== Computing □(φ·ψ̃) for double-log ===\")\npsi_dl = -sp.ln(1+t) - sp.ln(1+x)\nlam_val = sp.Rational(-1, 20)\nphi_dl = sp.exp(lam_val * psi_dl)\n\ntilde_sq_dl = sp.diff(psi_dl, t)**2 - c**2*sp.diff(psi_dl, x)**2\nproduct = phi_dl * tilde_sq_dl\nbox_product = sp.diff(product, t, 2) - c**2 * sp.diff(product, x, 2)\nbox_simplified = sp.simplify(box_product)\nprint(f\"□(φ·ψ̃) = {sp.factor(box_simplified)}\")\n\n# Also compute the first term (α-1)sλ□(φ□ψ) for α ≠ 1\nsq_dl = sp.diff(psi_dl, t, 2) - c**2 * sp.diff(psi_dl, x, 2)\nproduct2 = phi_dl * sq_dl\nbox_product2 = sp.diff(product2, t, 2) - c**2 * sp.diff(product2, x, 2)\nbox_simplified2 = sp.simplify(box_product2)\nprint(f\"□(φ·□ψ) = {sp.factor(box_simplified2)}\")\n\n# Check numerically: what's the sign of □(φ·ψ̃)?\nf = sp.lambdify((x, t, c), box_simplified, 'numpy')\nx_arr = np.linspace(0, 100, 50)\nt_arr = np.linspace(0, 100, 50)\nX, T = np.meshgrid(x_arr, t_arr)\nfor c_val in [0.5, 1.0, 2.0]:\n    Z = f(X, T, c_val)\n    print(f\"  c={c_val}: □(φ·ψ̃) min={np.min(Z):.6e}, max={np.max(Z):.6e}\")\n\nPYEOF","description":"Analyze L₂ term structure and sign of s","timeout":300000}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4427,"cache_read_input_tokens":129777,"cache_c