{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_018dHsFFH6UXrKyLFHcywvCL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01La4HWviFmreBE1CWy9zJtv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"source /home/cyanz/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate wave && python3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport sympy as sp\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Analyze the L₂ numerator structure for s=-1/100, λ=-1/20\n# The L₂ numerator (after extracting positive denominator) has terms like:\n# coefficient * ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) terms (these are the s³-order terms)\n# coefficient * constant terms (these are the s-order terms)\n#\n# From the output, the numerator has the form:\n# sum of terms like: A * R + B  where R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10)\n# and A can be negative while B is positive.\n#\n# The key question: for EVERY monomial c^a * t^b * x^d, is the total coefficient\n# of R small enough relative to the constant coefficient?\n\n# Let me extract the critical ratio.\n# Looking at the c^4*t^4 coefficient: -28*R + 5752500\n# This flips sign at R = 5752500/28 = 205446.4\n# R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) = 205446 means (1+t)(1+x) = 205446^10 ≈ 10^53\n\n# The c^2*t^2*x^2 coefficient: -1*R + 95000\n# Flips at R = 95000, meaning (1+t)(1+x) = 95000^10 ≈ 10^50\n\n# The x^4 coefficient: +29*R + 5752500\n# Always positive! ✓\n\n# So the z² coefficient of the quadratic in z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² is:\n# coefficient of c^4 * (1+t)^4: -28R + 5752500\n# This flips at R ≈ 205446, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) ≈ 10^53\n\n# Compare with Round 2 (s=-1, λ=-1/20): flipped at R ≈ 20.5, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) ≈ 10^13\n# And Round 3 (s=-1, λ=-1/50): flipped at R ≈ 50.5, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) ≈ 10^43\n\n# With s=-1/100, λ=-1/20: flips at R ≈ 205446, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) ≈ 10^53\n# This is MUCH better but still fails eventually.\n\n# The ratio: with s=-1, the R coefficient is O(1) and constant is O(N²).\n# With s=-1/M, the R coefficient is O(1/M²) (from s³ terms) and constant is O(N²/M) (from s terms).\n# So R_crit ~ N²M / (1/M²) = N²M³.\n# This means (1+t)(1+x) ~ (N²M³)^(N/2), which is HUGE.\n\n# But it's still FINITE. For any fixed M and N, there exists a failure point.\n# The problem statement says \"for all (x,t) in [0,∞) × [0,T]\" — T is FIXED!\n# So if we fix T and let x → ∞, we need L₂ ≥ 0 for all x ≥ 0 with t ∈ [0,T].\n\n# Wait... the domain is [0,∞) × [0,T] where T is GIVEN.\n# So t is BOUNDED by T! We don't need L₂ ≥ 0 for t → ∞, only for t ∈ [0,T]!\n\n# Let me re-read the problem...\n# \"for all (x, t) in [0,∞) × [0,T]\"\n# So t ∈ [0,T] is BOUNDED, only x goes to infinity.\n\n# For fixed t ≤ T and x → ∞:\n# R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) ≈ (1+x)^(1/10) · (1+t)^(1/10) → ∞\n# z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² → 0\n\n# So as x → ∞ with fixed t: z → 0 and R → ∞.\n# The quadratic f(z,R) at z=0 is just the constant term:\n# constant term: 29R + 5752500 > 0 always. ✓✓\n\n# As x → ∞, z → 0, so the quadratic ≈ constant term > 0. Good!\n\n# The problematic case was t → ∞ (with x fixed or t,x both large).\n# But t is bounded by T! So the failure at (1+t)(1+x) > 10^53 requires\n# (1+T)(1+x) > 10^53, i.e., x > 10^53/(1+T). This is a valid x.\n\n# Hmm, but with fixed T, as x → ∞: z → 0 and the quadratic value → constant term > 0.\n# The failure region was z large (c² large, t large relative to x).\n# With t ≤ T: z = c²(1+T)²/(1+x)² → 0 as x → ∞.\n# So for any fixed c and T, as x → ∞, L₂ → constant_term/denominator > 0.\n\n# The question is: for x in some intermediate range, can z be large enough to trigger\n# the negative z² term?\n# z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² ≤ c²(1+T)²/(1+x)² which is bounded and → 0 as x → ∞.\n# The maximum z is c²(1+T)² (at x=0, t=T).\n\n# So we need: f(c²(1+T)², R) ≥ 0 for all R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) ≥ 1.\n# The z² coefficient: -28R + 5752500.\n# If R < 205446, the quadratic is upward-opening and positive at z=0. \n# If R > 205446, it's downward-opening, with positive value at z=0.\n# The quadratic becomes negative at some z*. If c²(1+T)² < z*, we're fine.\n\n# Actually, the key insight is: we can make s and λ DEPEND ON c AND T!\n# s and λ are allowed to depend on c and T.\n# So we can set s = -1/(c²T² + 1) or something similar to ensure\n# the failure threshold is pushed beyond the actual domain.\n\nprint(\"KEY INSIGHT: s and λ can depend on c and T!\")\nprint(\"The failure point R_crit scales as O(M³N²) where s=-1/M, λ=-1/N\")\nprint(\"We need: c²(1+T)² ≤ z* where z* is the root of the quadratic at R_max\")\nprint()\nprint(\"Since R_max ~ ((1+T)·(1+x_max))^(1/10) where x_max corresponds to\")\nprint(\"the x value that maximizes z·R for fixed T and c,\")\nprint(\"we need to ensure the quadratic stays positive on the reachable (z,R) curve.\")\nprint()\n\n# Actually this is getting complicated. Let me think about it differently.\n# For the double-log with α=1:\n# L₂ = Term_B + Term_D + Term_E\n# Term_B = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃) which is O(|s|) and > 0\n# Term_D+E are O(|s|³) and involve the problematic φ³ growth\n#\n# The critical balance is |s|·(positive term) vs |s|³·(growing term)\n# For fixed λ, the crossover happens at R ~ 1/|s|².\n# With |s| = 1/(c²T²+1), R_crit ~ (c²T²+1)² which means\n# (1+t)(1+x) ~ ((c²T²+1)²)^(N/2) which is enormous.\n# But we need it to exceed c²(1+T)² at the maximal z in the domain.\n#\n# Actually, let me just try s = -1/(100*c^2) or similar, dependent on c.\n# If the problem allows s to depend on c and T, this is fine.\n\n# Let me test: s = -1/(c²·100), λ = -1/20\n# But in the subs_dict, s must be a concrete expression in c.\n\n# Let me try the most straightforward approach:\n# Use s as a parameter depending on c (which the problem allows).\n\nprint(\"Testing with s = -1/(100*c²), λ = -1/20\")\nx = sp.symbols('x', real=True)\nt = sp.symbols('t', real=True)  \nc = sp.symbols('c', positive=True)\nlam = sp.symbols('lam')\nalpha = sp.symbols('alpha')\ns = sp.symbols('s')\n\npsi = -sp.ln(1+t) - sp.ln(1+x)\nsubs_dict = {alpha: 1, s: -1/(100*c**2), lam: sp.Rational(-1, 20)}\n\n# Quick numerical check at the previously failing point\nfrom verify_engine import build_operators\nx_sym, t_sym, c_sym = sp.symbols('x t', real=True), sp.symbols('t', real=True), sp.symbols('c', positive=True)\n# Actually let me just use the verify engine directly\nimport importlib\nimport verify_engine\nimportlib.reload(verify_engine)\n\nresults, report = verify_engine.run_verification(psi, subs_dict)\n# Just print the summary\nfor line in report.split('\\n'):\n    if 'condition' in line.lower() or 'SUMMARY' in line or 'PASS' in line:\n        print(line)\n\nPYEOF","description":"Analyze domain constraints and test c-dependent s","timeout":600000}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4084,"cache_read_input_tokens":140216,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4084,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78ba00bf-7c93-4882-8607-6977578eab2d","uuid":"465ac448-81f8-403b-b2c4-a6d92bfe96e7"}
